WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.740
Every cell with a nucleus, from yeast to you, runs its cycle on one engine.

2
00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:12.620
The engine is a kinase called a CDK, a cyclin-dependent kinase.

3
00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:14.540
Alone, it does nothing.

4
00:00:15.380 --> 00:00:19.080
It switches on only when a partner protein, a cyclin, is bound.

5
00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:23.940
The cell builds cyclin up, the kinase switches on, and the next step begins.

6
00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:29.200
Then the cell destroys the cyclin, the kinase switches off, and the cycle resets.

7
00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:30.200
. . . .

8
00:00:30.233 --> 00:00:32.693
Different cyclins rise at different times.

9
00:00:33.733 --> 00:00:39.433
Cyclin D works in G1, cyclin E drives the move from G1 into S, and cyclin B with

10
00:00:39.433 --> 00:00:41.653
CDK1 drives mitosis.

11
00:00:42.573 --> 00:00:44.893
That is the usual pattern, not a strict rule.

12
00:00:45.917 --> 00:00:47.297
The engine has brakes.

13
00:00:48.337 --> 00:00:53.777
Wee1 puts an inhibitory phosphate on CDK1, and Cdc25 takes it off.

14
00:00:54.677 --> 00:00:57.217
Inhibitor proteins can also bind and block.

15
00:00:58.437 --> 00:01:03.517
Active CDK1 switches on its activator and switches off its brake, so once mitosis

16
00:01:03.517 --> 00:01:05.377
starts, it goes all the way.

17
00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:08.700
Checkpoints decide when the brakes stay on.

18
00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:13.740
In G1, a cell commits only if it has enough nutrients and growth factors.

19
00:01:14.680 --> 00:01:21.660
If DNA is damaged or not fully copied, checkpoint signals act on Wee1 and Cdc25, and

20
00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:23.020
the cell waits in G2.

21
00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:28.820
In mitosis, a checkpoint holds back destruction until every chromosome is attached.

22
00:01:29.883 --> 00:01:32.243
Destruction makes the cycle run one way.

23
00:01:33.143 --> 00:01:39.163
A ubiquitin ligase, the APC/C, tags securin, so sister chromatids can separate, and

24
00:01:39.163 --> 00:01:41.363
then cyclin B, which ends mitosis.

25
00:01:42.403 --> 00:01:46.643
A cyclin that cannot be destroyed holds a frog egg extract in mitosis.

26
00:01:47.583 --> 00:01:49.103
Making cyclin drives entry.

27
00:01:50.063 --> 00:01:51.283
Destroying it permits exit.

28
00:01:52.267 --> 00:01:54.367
The engine explains an old puzzle.

29
00:01:55.387 --> 00:01:59.947
Fuse a cell that is copying its DNA with one that has not started, and the second

30
00:01:59.947 --> 00:02:01.167
nucleus starts early.

31
00:02:02.147 --> 00:02:05.227
Fuse it with one that has finished, and that nucleus refuses.

32
00:02:06.207 --> 00:02:08.007
A genome is copied once per cycle.

33
00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:10.980
Three lines of work found the engine.

34
00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:14.400
Frog oocytes gave an activity, MPF.

35
00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:17.260
Yeast mutants gave the gene Cdc2.

36
00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:19.440
Sea urchins gave cyclin.

37
00:02:20.180 --> 00:02:26.260
Then the lines met: MPF is Cdc2, now called CDK1, bound to cyclin B.
